// Shop content — what the player can buy, and what it costs (GAMEPLAY.md
// §1's core loop and §4's "equipment purchases *are* the tech tree"). Data,
// never hardcoded in sim_core, exactly like `recipes.ron` and
// `machines.ron`; the loader is `sim_core::sim::shop::ShopCatalog`.
//
// `schema_version` follows ARCHITECTURE.md §4's data-file versioning rule:
// the loader rejects a mismatched version rather than silently misreading a
// changed shape, the same way the other two data files' loaders do.
//
// WHAT THIS FILE IS FOR. GAMEPLAY.md §1 is explicit that there is no
// separate currency or tech-point resource: "money buys equipment,
// equipment unlocks sauces, sauces satisfy contracts, contracts pay money."
// This file is the "money buys equipment" half of that sentence made
// concrete — a catalog of every buildable kind together with its price in
// the same money `Economy` already counts. Buying a kind credits one to the
// player's owned count of it; nothing more yet.
//
// WHAT IT IS *NOT* FOR YET ("Shop & Inventory" epic, ticket 1 of 2). Today
// every machine in the chain still exists unconditionally from tick 0 —
// `World::new` builds the whole fixed line whether or not anything has been
// purchased. This ticket is deliberately additive: the shop, the prices and
// the owned counts are real and fully simulated, but nothing consumes an
// owned count yet. A later epic rewires placement to actually spend this
// inventory; until then a purchase is a real money transaction whose result
// is a number in `shop_snapshot_json`, not a machine appearing on the floor.
//
// KIND IDS. An `id` here is a *buildable kind* id, which is a superset of
// `data/machines.ron`'s machine kinds: every kind that file defines appears
// here (the loader's `validate_covers` checks exactly that, so adding a
// machine kind without pricing it is an error rather than a thing the
// player can never buy), plus the two pieces of infrastructure that are not
// machines at all — a belt tile and a pipe tile. Those two are priced *per
// tile*, because that is the unit a player places them in; everything else
// is priced per building.
//
// PRICES ARE PLACEHOLDERS. GAMEPLAY.md §10 puts exact numeric balance
// ("ingredient costs, contract payouts, equipment prices, wage rates")
// explicitly in a future balancing pass, not here, and this project is
// pre-alpha. What these numbers *do* encode is a deliberate shape, so the
// catalog reads sensibly even before it is balanced:
//
//   1. Infrastructure is cheap enough to lay by the dozen; a building costs
//      an order of magnitude more than a tile of belt.
//   2. A building's price tracks how much machine it is — the same instinct
//      `machines.ron` uses to decide footprints. The 2x2 cooking vat that
//      runs the chain's longest process is the most expensive thing here;
//      the crusher, the simplest machine on the line, is the cheapest.
//   3. Everything is scaled against the one economic number that already
//      exists: `economy.rs`'s hardcoded contract pays 50 per delivery. So a
//      belt tile is small change, a mid-chain machine is a couple of
//      deliveries, and the cooking vat is a genuine save-up.
//
// Order matters only for presentation: the loader preserves this file's
// order and `shop_snapshot_json` reports the catalog in it, so this is the
// order a build menu lists things in. It runs infrastructure first, then the
// two buffers, then the chain's machines in chain order.
(
    schema_version: 1,
    entries: [
        // --- Infrastructure, priced per tile ---
        ShopEntry(
            id: "belt",
            price: 5,
        ),
        // Slightly dearer than a belt tile: a pipe run is stainless and
        // carries a fluid (UI_UX.md §2b point 6), where a belt is rubber.
        ShopEntry(
            id: "pipe",
            price: 8,
        ),

        // --- Buffers: placed buildings with no recipe (see machines.ron) ---
        ShopEntry(
            id: "hopper",
            price: 40,
        ),
        // Dearer than the hopper for the same reason a pipe is dearer than
        // a belt — a pressure vessel is more than a bin.
        ShopEntry(
            id: "fluid_tank",
            price: 60,
        ),

        // --- Chain machines, in `data/recipes.ron` order ---
        // The line's simplest machine (a hopper and a screw) and its
        // cheapest.
        ShopEntry(
            id: "crusher",
            price: 75,
        ),
        ShopEntry(
            id: "mixer",
            price: 100,
        ),
        // The most expensive thing in the catalog, on purpose: the only 2x2
        // kind, running the chain's longest process. GAMEPLAY.md §4 treats
        // the cooker/kettle as a real capital purchase, and this is it.
        ShopEntry(
            id: "cooking_vat",
            price: 200,
        ),
        // GAMEPLAY.md §4 groups bottling with the reduction step as one
        // equipment tier, so it is priced in the vat's neighbourhood rather
        // than with the cheap front end.
        ShopEntry(
            id: "bottler",
            price: 120,
        ),
        ShopEntry(
            id: "packer",
            price: 90,
        ),
    ],
)
